      "id": 1,
      "text": "When the Lawrence Railroad reached South Paris on June 8, 1850, the village quickly grew into a bustling mill town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Paris%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Little Androscoggin River ran through South Paris, supplying water power that drove the town’s early mills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Paris%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Even after manufacturing faded, South Paris kept much of its Victorian‑era architecture, giving the town a historic charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Paris%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A must‑see is the McLaughlin Garden & Homestead, a four‑season perennial garden surrounding a preserved New England farmhouse.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://winslowmaine.org/exploring-the-hidden-gem-a-comprehensive-guide-to-south-paris-maine-unveiling-the-charms-and-delights-of-this-quaint-new-england-town"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s Hamlin Memorial Library sits inside the 1822 Old Stone Jail, a historic building that once housed the county jail.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
